pub fn ls_remote_reports_termination_not_success_note() -> String {
    thread_local! {
        static CACHED: String = {
            "IT REPORTS THE EXIT CODE AND NOT A SUCCESS BOOL, WHICH IS A NARROWING FROM ITS FIRST CUT. That cut declared `success: Bool from \"exit_success\"` beside `exit_code`, and the two are not independent: the seed derives exit_success as exactly `exit_code == 0` (v1_interpreter.rs, the shell dispatch output-source match), so the pair carried one fact twice.\\n\\nCARRYING IT TWICE WAS NOT MERELY REDUNDANT, IT MADE A CONTRADICTION WRITABLE. A caller of the deciding function could pass success true beside a nonzero exit code, and the function branched on success - so a failed advertisement read as a successful one and the branch reported unpublished rather than unreadable. That is the ignorance-as-answer conflation this operation exists to prevent, reachable through the operation own output shape. Dropping the Bool makes the contradictory pair unrepresentable rather than merely unlikely, which is why this is construction and not a check.\\n\\nWHICH LAYER OWNS SUCCESS, since something still has to answer it: the caller EXPECTATION does. Process termination, operation observation, caller expectation and log disposition are four facts, and a probe may legitimately use a nonzero exit as an ANSWER rather than a verdict - so an operation that pre-judges its own exit code as success has decided something only its caller can. See gunbc.plans.transport_argv_anemia_dissolution, which measures 148 sibling operations in dag/extdeps still carrying the Bool while 51 preserve the integer. This operation is the first consumer of that correction, not a local exception.".to_string()
        };
    }
    CACHED.with(|c: &String| c.clone())
}

pub fn ls_remote_carries_no_exit_block_note() -> String {
    thread_local! {
        static CACHED: String = {
            "WHY LsRemoteHeads DECLARES NO `exit` BLOCK WHILE EVERY SIBLING OPERATION DOES, which is the first thing a reader will notice. The `exit` arms are INERT AT RUN TIME - established by reading the seed, not assumed. The shell dispatch in v1_interpreter.rs maps declared output sources (`stdout`, `stderr`, `exit_success`, `exit_code`, `stdout_lines`) onto the process result and returns a record; it never consults the operation's exit arms and never raises on a nonzero exit. The only consumer of exit_props anywhere outside the parser is v1_compiler_emit_rust.rs, which round-trips them back to source so the self-host fixed point holds. So an exit arm is a declaration that is parsed, re-emitted, and never realized.\\n\\nThat makes this operation's OUTPUTS the realized authority for its outcome: a caller learns whether the read succeeded from `exit_code`/`stderr`, which are values it can match on. (It carried a `success` Bool too until this operation narrowed - see ls_remote_reports_termination_not_success_note directly above, which is the authority for why. Left unfixed, this sentence would have been a stale citation inside the very PR that made it false.) Adding an exit block beside them would be a second representation of the same fact where only one of the two executes - and since this operation is new, authoring one knowing it is inert would be minting a fresh declared-but-unrealized instance rather than inheriting an existing one. The corpus-wide inertness of exit blocks is a real finding and a separate correction; it is not fixed by making one operation diverge from a hundred siblings, and it is not made worse by declining to add the hundred-and-first.".to_string()
        };
    }
    CACHED.with(|c: &String| c.clone())
}

